I can focus on yearmonth to compute both total consumption and January 2012 consumption with this query:  

SELECT SUM(Consumption), SUM(CASE WHEN Date = '201201' THEN Consumption ELSE 0 END) FROM yearmonth WHERE CustomerID = '38508';  

This will return one row with two columns.

My query is going to look something like this:

SELECT CAST(SUM(CASE WHEN bond.bond_type = '-' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS REAL) / COUNT(atom.atom_id) from molecule JOIN bond ON molecule.molecule_id = bond.molecule_id JOIN atom ON molecule.molecule_id = atom.molecule_id WHERE molecule.label = '+';

I should use COUNT(atom.atom_id) instead of COUNT(*), and I can potentially use SUM(bond_type='-') if SQLite handles boolean.

The SQL query should be: 

SELECT DISTINCT f.name FROM cards AS c INNER JOIN foreign_data AS f ON c.uuid = f.uuid WHERE c.name = 'Ancestor''s Chosen' AND f.language = 'Korean';

If a Korean version exists, it will return the foreign name; otherwise, there will be no results.

I might produce a query using subqueries for clarity: 

SELECT 1.0 * (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM schools WHERE County='Orange' AND DOC='54') / NULLIF((SELECT COUNT(*) FROM schools WHERE County='Orange' AND DOC='52'), 0);

This query should return the ratio correctly since multiplying by 1.0 ensures a float in SQLite.

My initial thought is to use: 

SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT d.driverId) FROM drivers d JOIN lapTimes l ON d.driverId=l.driverId WHERE d.nationality='French' AND l.milliseconds < 120000;

I considered using COUNT with the driver's name, but driverId seems better.
